{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1413592-thoughts-on-making-rotes-things-you-buy","id":1413592,"title":"Thoughts on making Rotes things you buy","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-09-14T11:40:28-07:00","id":1413592,"title":"Thoughts on making Rotes things you buy","content":"So I\u2019m assuming they have conversions for rotes in the Mage translation guide?<br /> <br /> What are thoughts on making Rotes things say you buy with experience and they make that specific rote less difficult, or maybe you get more bang for your buck with each quintessence spent?<br /> <br /> Just felt weird they weren\u2019t like things you fully learn, they seemed more like secret ideas.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-14T12:23:15-07:00","id":1413601,"title":"","content":"In <i>Awakening</i>, Entropy got split into the Death and Fate spheres. Other spheres are the same.<br /> <br /> <i>Enlightened Grimoire</i> by Charles Siegel has an optional rote system, including improvements to the rotes. You buy them with XP, though there is a system to give each character some rotes to begin with and starting characters with a high Library or Mentor rating get more points for rotes to reflect they had more options to learn them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/249171/Enlightened-Grimoire?term=enlightened+grim\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/images/1/249171.jpg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/249171/Enlightened-Grimoire?term=enlightened+grim\" target=\"_blank\"> Enlightened Grimoire - White Wolf | Storytellers Vault </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/249171/Enlightened-Grimoire?term=enlightened+grim\" target=\"_blank\"> https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/249171/Enlightened-Grimoire?term=enlightened+grim </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> Enlightened Grimoire - Twenty-Five Years of Magick Over the last twenty-five years of Mage: The Ascension, spells have been scattered like trea </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Also, Enlightened Grimoire is a compilation of rotes in published Mage books as of the time Charles finished it. It groups the rotes, gives you the original source with page numbers and summarizes the rotes. So, it's not particularly original, but it's well worth the effort for someone wanting to see the myriad rotes in Mage without going through all the books. PDF search works very nicely--I look up a rote in there regularly for my game.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-14T12:49:27-07:00","id":1413606,"title":"","content":"My own approach to Rotes is much more radical; but that's because I see them as a way to replace Hedge Magic with an all-Mage take on Sleepers having access to Magick: Rotes become something that anyone with the right Focus can learn and use \u2014 though there are drawbacks if you don't have sufficient Spheres or/and Arete to perform them naturally. I model their cost off of the cost of an equivalent Enhancement. <br /> <br /> Meanwhile, MRev declared that fast-casting was a penalty for not working Magic<s>k</s> by Rote. I tend to turn that around: if you've learned a Rote, but have sufficient Spheres to perform the Effect without it, the Rote is good for a -1 Difficulty.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-14T13:22:55-07:00","id":1413608,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dataweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1413592-thoughts-on-making-rotes-things-you-buy?p=1413606#post1413606\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">My own approach to Rotes is much more radical; but that's because I see them as a way to replace Hedge Magic with an all-Mage take on Sleepers having access to Magick: Rotes become something that anyone with the right Focus can learn and use \u2014 though there are drawbacks if you don't have sufficient Spheres or/and Arete to perform them naturally. I model their cost off of the cost of an equivalent Enhancement. <br /> <br /> Meanwhile, MRev declared that fast-casting was a penalty for not working Magic<s>k</s> by Rote. I tend to turn that around: if you've learned a Rote, but have sufficient Spheres to perform the Effect without it, the Rote is good for a -1 Difficulty.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was thinking about your idea when I was tossing this around in my head. What was the cost you were imagining for mortals to learn rotes. I was thinking -1 as a basic but I\u2019m not sure how much exp that would be worth.<br /> <br /> I was thinking of keeping paths and rotes.<br /> <br /> <br /> What are thoughts about totes allowing Mages to cast the rote with an appropriate attribute plus ability? Or for rotes you can add the highest sphere\u2019s rating to your arete?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-14T15:26:43-07:00","id":1413626,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Eldagusto</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1413592-thoughts-on-making-rotes-things-you-buy?p=1413608#post1413608\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was thinking about your idea when I was tossing this around in my head. What was the cost you were imagining for mortals to learn rotes. I was thinking -1 as a basic but I\u2019m not sure how much exp that would be worth.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's an option in M20 for buying Backgrounds with experience points; I would use that as a guideline as to the experience cost of a Rote: figure what Background rating for an Enhancement that can produce the same Effect, and say that the Rote's experience cost is <i>at least</i> as much as the experience cost for said Background; probably more, especially if Rotes lack some of the problems that Enhancements have. <br /> <br /> I'd make that available to <i>anyone</i> who has a compatible Focus, whether or not they're Awakened; that said, I'm of the opinion that the average Sleeper doesn't have a Focus; for the most part, you need to be Awakened to have a Focus, with Extraordinary Citizens and their mystical counterparts being the most noteworthy exceptions. That is, in order to gain a Focus and thus be able to learn Rotes as a Sleeper, you need to go through some sort of initiation into the supernatural world that makes them into true believers of the worldview that the Focus represents. <br /> <br /> Not sure what you mean by \u201c-1 as a basic\u201d. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Eldagusto</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1413592-thoughts-on-making-rotes-things-you-buy?p=1413608#post1413608\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was thinking of keeping paths and rotes.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't object to Hedge Magic; I only object to considering it to be somehow linked to Awakened Magick. That is, I prefer to treat Hedge Magic and Awakened Magick as similar but distinct things, in much the same way that vampiric thaumaturgy or werewolf rites or mummy Hekau are similar in appearance to Awakened Magick channeled through appropriate Foci but are based on fundamentally different metaphysics under the hood: and <i>because</i> Path Magic only <i>resembles</i> Awakened Magick but isn't actually the same thing, there's no need to try to force Paths into being a lesser subset of Awakened Magick, which is arguably a \u201csquare peg in a round hole\u201d exercise. <br /> <br /> But if you want a Path-like structure to learning Rotes, you can insist on purchasing Rotes in a more structured manner: e.g., you can't have more two-dot Rotes than you have one-dot Rotes, etc. That said, I've considered the option, but ultimately decided against it. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Eldagusto</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1413592-thoughts-on-making-rotes-things-you-buy?p=1413608#post1413608\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What are thoughts about rotes allowing Mages to cast the rote with an appropriate attribute plus ability? Or for rotes you can add the highest sphere\u2019s rating to your arete?</div> </div> </div> </div>There are already rules for Abilities Enhancing Magick; they involve making an Ability roll land using the resulting successes to reduce the difficulty of the subsequent Arete roll. I wouldn't object to replacing that with an \u201cAbilities Enhancing Magick\u201d rule that instead adds Ability dice directly to the casting pool; but I'm not sure I'd want to tie that into Rotes. Just leave it as a general \u201cif you have a suitable Ability and can work on into the casting of the Effect, you get the bonus dice\u201d. <br /> <br /> On the other hand, if you want Rotes to provide more of a benefit than a -1 Difficulty when you have sufficient Spheres for the Effect, I could see saying that the benefit is that you get to roll Arete+Sphere instead of just Arete, in place of getting a difficulty reduction. Just note that combining this with the above Ability Enhancing Magick variation can potentially give you upward of 20 dice in your pool: 10 from Arete, 5 from the highest Sphere, and 5 from the Ability.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-14T15:55:27-07:00","id":1413634,"title":"","content":"Sorry I meant basic -1 difficulty.<br /> <br /> Oh I meant ability+attribute replacing the arete as an idea to show how rotes work, I believe that is what awakened does, and fits as a method for how mortals cast.<br /> <br /> I just am not a fan of the idea rotes are secrets of specific traditions when all they are is examples of sphere uses and spheres combos.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-14T16:01:26-07:00","id":1413638,"title":"","content":"The thing is, rotes are a way to do magic that has been codified. They are specific spells, so even if you do not want to make the spheres any different across the groups of mages who can cast them, a Forces 2/Life 2 'make me invisible' effect should have different flavors for the Order of Hermes (they would call it Cloak of Hermes) than for a Virtual Adept (would be some kind of computer image thing) or a Dreamspeaker (may call on a spirit or may tie to their spiritual essence being hidden by the elements).<br /> <br /> Maybe call them recipes. So, the 'personal invisibility' spell above just has a few flavors but someone who is a True Mage recognizes that well, they are just seasoned to taste with different spices. That would make them more like Hedge Magic paths.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-14T16:16:37-07:00","id":1413642,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>baakyocalder</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1413592-thoughts-on-making-rotes-things-you-buy?p=1413601#post1413601\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In <i>Awakening</i>, Entropy got split into the Death and Fate spheres. Other spheres are the same.<br /> <br /> <i>Enlightened Grimoire</i> by Charles Siegel has an optional rote system, including improvements to the rotes. You buy them with XP, though there is a system to give each character some rotes to begin with and starting characters with a high Library or Mentor rating get more points for rotes to reflect they had more options to learn them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/249171/Enlightened-Grimoire?term=enlightened+grim\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/images/1/249171.jpg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/249171/Enlightened-Grimoire?term=enlightened+grim\" target=\"_blank\"> Enlightened Grimoire - White Wolf | Storytellers Vault </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/249171/Enlightened-Grimoire?term=enlightened+grim\" target=\"_blank\"> https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/249171/Enlightened-Grimoire?term=enlightened+grim </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> Enlightened Grimoire - Twenty-Five Years of Magick Over the last twenty-five years of Mage: The Ascension, spells have been scattered like trea </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Also, Enlightened Grimoire is a compilation of rotes in published Mage books as of the time Charles finished it. It groups the rotes, gives you the original source with page numbers and summarizes the rotes. So, it's not particularly original, but it's well worth the effort for someone wanting to see the myriad rotes in Mage without going through all the books. PDF search works very nicely--I look up a rote in there regularly for my game.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>That\u2019s funny I was listening to the Mage podcast and when they talked about a book called Enlightened Grimoires and that made me think of messing with rotes, and when I posted this I hit the podcast episode dedicated to that book.<br /> <br /> So are you suggesting recipes are what mortals would call rotes or just replacing rotes as a term in general.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-14T17:15:48-07:00","id":1413660,"title":"","content":"Mage: The Podcast is pretty interesting--and Charles Siegel has been on a few times to talk about his work. It's well worth listening to Mage: the Podcast if you're interested in the topic.<br /> <br /> My point of comparing rotes to recipes is to say they are basically ways to use the same basic ingredients and some flavor to make the same general thing. Mortals don't know about rotes, but they may believe in magical spells being cast a certain way or technology being used in a certain way to yield certain results. Me, I don't like rotes so much in Mage. They remind me of spells in D&amp;D and other games, and most players don't really do more than figure out which spells are OP. They skip the roleplaying of the spell components and other items.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13680-baakyocalder","name":"baakyocalder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13680_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"353"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-14T18:32:02-07:00","id":1413678,"title":"","content":"I've developed a simple rote system for my games. Rotes, for my purpose, are codified magical applications, the sorts of spells you'd find passed down from master to apprentice or effects the mage PC has created and practiced.<br /> <br /> At the start, players get a number of Rote Points equal to...huh, I can't find it, but it was something like Arete + highest Sphere. Then at the end of every story, each PC would gain an additional rote point (Hermetics get 2--all Trads have their little bonuses). One rote point buys one rote. That's it. Xp can also be spent on additional rotes on a 1 for 1 basis. The benefit of a rote is also simple: 1s do not subtract successes (though the caster can still botch if no successes are rolled). <br /> <br /> As an additional house role, players often roll an Attribute + Ability to effectively use their focus or otherwise in conjunction with a casting. 3 successes adds an additional die to the Arete roll, while 5+ successes adds a success. Thus, an exceptional performance will forward the mage's purpose more than a mediocre one.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12765-otto-d-fey","name":"Otto D'Fey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12765_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"44"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-14T19:29:13-07:00","id":1413687,"title":"","content":"Rotes are very much <i>not</i> just examples of what you can do with the Spheres; Focus absolutely should be a consideration as well. Rotes ought to have the following parts:<br /> <br /> 1. What is the Focus? <br /> 2. What are the Sphere requirements?<br /> 3. What is the underlying Effect?<br /> 4. What level of Feat is it? <br /> <br /> The Focus is mainly a matter of Practice, although Paradigm may factor in as well. (Obviously, I'm using the M20 terminology here.) Instruments and Abilities <i>definitely</i> factor in, with the Practice not only determining which Instruments and Abilities are appropriate, but also providing the framework needed to decide what the mage does with the Instruments involved and the choice of Ability determining how well the mage is at doing that. If you can't come up with an activity for the Rote that makes sense for your Focus, you can't perform the Rote. And if you're using a Rote, you cannot Work Without Focus and you must use the Instruments associated with that Rote (though if you've raised your Arete high enough to have discarded an Instrument that the Rote requires, you still get the -1 Difficulty for using an Instrument when you don't need to). This is the main price you must pay (other than whatever experience cost is involved) to gain access to whatever the benefits of a Rote happen to be. <br /> <br /> Spheres, I don't have much to say about<b></b>: to my mind, they're mainly a bookkeeping abstraction, good for helping determine the experience cost of the Rote and for determining what it takes to improvise the Effect that the Rote embodies. That; and like I said, I wouldn't be averse to the notion that if you have enough Sphere dots to improvise the underlying Effect, you get to add your Sphere's dots to the casting pool. <br /> <br /> The underlying Effect <i>is</i> what it's all about, ultimately; but to my mind, the most important element to consider isn't \u201cwhich Spheres are needed to do this?\u201d but rather \u201chow does the Focus apply to this?\u201d Spheres are less about what you can do and more about what you can improvise; Rotes, as I envision them, provide another way to determine what you can do, independent of what you can improvise. Note that in earlier editions of the game, this emphasis on the importance of Focus would have translated to Rotes being linked to Traditions, Conventions, or Crafts, because before M20, your Practice was determined by your allegiance. As of M20, your allegiance tends to put restrictions on what your Focus can be (e.g., the Celestial Chorus does <i>not</i> include any atheists in its ranks, save for \u201ccrisis of faith\u201d storylines which tend to be resolved by the mage in question either reaffirming her faith or leaving the Tradition); but no allegiance holds a monopoly on any Focus, and ultimately it's your personal Focus that determines which Rotes you can and cannot learn and use. <br /> <br /> Feats need to be considered (explicitly, IMHO) because not every Effect is going to be \u201cachieve two successes to do this\u201d (a Standard Feat). Note that the Feat determines more than just how many successes are required, although that's important; the more successes are required, the more likely you'll need to make an extended roll too achieve the necessary successes. But on top of that, a large enough Feat increases the Difficulty of the casting (M20 p.503, <i>General Circumstances</i>: Outlandish to Godlike Feat entry) and, if you need to do an extended casting, optionally increases how long it will take (pp.541\u2013542, <i>Optional Rule: Rite, Ceremony, and Great Work</i>). Note that radical increases in the size of the casting pool significant increase how big of a Feat you can have a reasonable chance of achieving with a single roll, bypassing the need to perform it as a Ritual. <br /> <br /> Which brings me to your suggestion to make the pool for a Rote an Attribute+Ability roll. Attributes start at two dots, and are cheaper to raise than Arete (half the cost, in fact), and Abilities are even cheaper than that (a quarter of the cost); and both are much more widely useful than Arete is. This would mean that if Rotes go to Attribute+Ability pools instead of Arete pools, they need to be <i>especially</i> expensive to reflect their significant benefit. My proposed xp cost was based on the assumption that the pool for a Rote would be no bigger than Arete, and possibly smaller (though no smaller than the highest required Sphere rating). Being able to add the Sphere rating to the pool can potentially double the poop's size; but Spheres are right as difficult to increase as Arete is, which reins in how potent of a boost this is likely to be. I'd still want to increase the xp cost of a Rote; just not as much as I would for replacing the dice pool with an Attribute+Ability pool.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-16T13:11:48-07:00","id":1414023,"title":"","content":"I liked Awakening's approach to rotes and that's always one option. Ascension always seemed to treat Rotes (slightly) as a difficulty modifier (no fast cast penalty if its a rote?) but you could expand that to reduce difficulties on rolls (if you wanted to simulate linear magic you could say 'develop' a rote where it reduces the difficulty by one for a fixed number of XP added to the cost of learning the rote, but it has to be performed in exactly that manner.) You might also consider treating it as 'vulgar without witnesses' which would seem to bring it closer to linear magic's reduced severity.)<br /> <br /> Atribute+ability could work I suppose (it works for sorcery AND Dark Ages magic followed similar with foundation+Pillar) but given those inspirations I think it would reflect a magical path that is much, much more limited than 'true' sphere-based magic. Something you'd find in some sort of cult or a group of Orphans (someone with debased knowledge rather than the formalized approach of the Traditions and more prominent Crafts.) I vaguely recall that the Mind's Eye Theatre had a similar (optional) rule for hedge magic somewhere (free-casting? I can't remember exactly.)<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25533-mister_dunpeal","name":"Mister_Dunpeal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"308"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-17T07:32:00-07:00","id":1414199,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mister_Dunpeal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1413592-thoughts-on-making-rotes-things-you-buy?p=1414023#post1414023\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I liked Awakening's approach to rotes and that's always one option. Ascension always seemed to treat Rotes (slightly) as a difficulty modifier (no fast cast penalty if its a rote?) but you could expand that to reduce difficulties on rolls (if you wanted to simulate linear magic you could say 'develop' a rote where it reduces the difficulty by one for a fixed number of XP added to the cost of learning the rote, but it has to be performed in exactly that manner.) You might also consider treating it as 'vulgar without witnesses' which would seem to bring it closer to linear magic's reduced severity.)</div> </div> </div> </div> Awakening's approach is two-fold: there are Rotes (which can only be created by Masters) that let you add an Ability to the pool and give it the Rote Quality; and there are Praxes (which anyone can create) which make it more likely to achieve critical successes. Awakening doesn't have variable difficulties, but it has a few \u201cdice trick\u201d options (such as a \u201c10 again\u201d rule, a \u201c9 again\u201d rule, a Rote Quality or \u201creroll the failed dice once\u201d, and \u201ccritical success on 3 successes instead of 5\u201d) which serve a similar purpose of enhancing the effectiveness of a pool without increasing its size. I think what Ascension has traditionally called Rotes is most analogous to Awakening's Praxes. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mister_Dunpeal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1413592-thoughts-on-making-rotes-things-you-buy?p=1414023#post1414023\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Atribute+ability could work I suppose (it works for sorcery AND Dark Ages magic followed similar with foundation+Pillar) but given those inspirations I think it would reflect a magical path that is much, much more limited than 'true' sphere-based magic. Something you'd find in some sort of cult or a group of Orphans (someone with debased knowledge rather than the formalized approach of the Traditions and more prominent Crafts.) I vaguely recall that the Mind's Eye Theatre had a similar (optional) rule for hedge magic somewhere (free-casting? I can't remember exactly.)</div> </div> </div> </div>The Foundation and Pillars system uses a pool that's essentially Arete+Sphere; and given the xp cost associated with Spheres, that's not as game breaking as Attribute+Ability. Sorcery can get away with Attribute+Ability because the way spellcasting in the Hedge Magic rules works is different from the way crafting an Effect in the Awakened Magick rules work; they really are different magic systems that only have a surface resemblance.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}